<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Sparse matrix–vector multiplication (SpMV) based on CSR format and parallelized via OpenMP target directives.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">With regard to the HPCCG benchmark, the OpenMP target implementation for GPUs is straightforward for the three type of kernels found in this application as shown in Code <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4.2 OpenMP offload ‣ 4 Task-aware libraries for heterogeneous computing ‣ A Task-based Data-flow Methodology for Programming Heterogeneous Systems with Multiple Accelerator APIs©2026. This manuscript version is made available under the CC BY-NC-ND 4.0 license https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> corresponding to the sparse matrix–vector multiplication. Note that this code excerpt works on horizontal tiles of the sparse matrix <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">A</span> and consecutive regions of the output vector <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">d_Ax</span> by using the starting row index (<span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">rowIdx</span>) and row block sizes (<span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">rowBsA</span>) variables hence allowing us to combine both target and task directives within the same code.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>SYCL</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">SYCL kernels are submitted to a device queue using the <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">parallel_for</span> construct, and operate over multi-dimensional ranges to expose parallelism. As shown in Code <a href="#S4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 4.3 SYCL ‣ 4 Task-aware libraries for heterogeneous computing ‣ A Task-based Data-flow Methodology for Programming Heterogeneous Systems with Multiple Accelerator APIs©2026. This manuscript version is made available under the CC BY-NC-ND 4.0 license https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, the sparse matrix–vector multiplication (SpMV) kernel used in HPCCG is offloaded using a 1D range, where each work-item processes a separate row of the matrix. The kernel traverses the compressed sparse row (CSR) data structures to compute the dot product of each row with the input vector, storing the result in the corresponding entry of the output vector.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="S4.F8.2" class="ltx_listing ltx_lstlisting ltx_listing"><div class="ltx_listing_data"><a href="data:text/plain;base64," download="">⬇</a></div>
